586–590 .\n679\t \n680\t External Links: Document ,\n681\t ISSN 2958-1796 \n682\t \n683\t Cited by: §1 .\n684\t \n685\t \n686\t \n687\t R. Roy, J. Raiman, S. Lee, T. Ene, R. Kirby, S. Kim, J. Kim, and B. Catanzaro (2026) \n688\t PersonaPlex: Voice and role control for full duplex conversational speech models .\n689\t \n690\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2602.06053 .\n691\t \n692\t External Links: Link \n693\t \n694\t Cited by: §1 .\n695\t \n696\t \n697\t \n698\t D. Schlangen and G. Skantze (2009) \n699\t A general, abstract model of incremental dialogue processing .\n700\t \n701\t In Proceedings of the 12th Conference of the European Chapter of the ACL (EACL 2009) ,\n702\t \n703\t Athens, Greece , pp. 710–718 .\n704\t \n705\t External Links: Link \n706\t \n707\t Cited by: §1 .\n708\t \n709\t \n710\t \n711\t T. Shiwa, T. Kanda, M. Imai, H. Ishiguro, and N. Hagita (2009) \n712\t How quickly should a communication robot respond? delaying strategies and habituation effects .\n713\t \n714\t International Journal of Social Robotics 1 ( 2 ), pp. 141–155 .\n715\t \n716\t External Links: Document \n717\t \n718\t Cited by: §2.1 .\n719\t \n720\t \n721\t \n722\t G. Skantze and B. Irfan (2025) \n723\t Applying general turn-taking models to conversational human-robot interaction .\n724\t \n725\t In Proceedings of the 2025 ACM/IEEE International Conference on Human-Robot Interaction ,\n726\t \n727\t HRI ’25 , pp. 859–868 .\n728\t \n729\t External Links: Link \n730\t \n731\t Cited by: §1 .\n732\t \n733\t \n734\t \n735\t G. Skantze (2021) \n736\t Turn-taking in conversational systems and human-robot interaction: a review .\n737\t \n738\t Computer Speech & Language 67 , pp. 101178 .\n739\t \n740\t External Links: Document \n741\t \n742\t Cited by: §1 .\n743\t \n744\t \n745\t \n746\t B. Warner, A. Chaffin, B. Clavié, O. Weller, O. Hallström, S. Taghadouini, A. Gallagher, R. Biswas, F. Ladhak, T. Aarsen, N. Cooper, G. Adams, J. Howard, and I. Poli (2024) \n747\t Smarter, better, faster, longer: a modern bidirectional encoder for fast, memory efficient, and long context finetuning and inference .\n748\t \n749\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2412.13663 .\n750\t \n751\t External Links: Document ,\n752\t Link \n753\t \n754\t Cited by: §2.2 .\n755\t \n756\t \n757\t \n758\t J. Yang, Y. Fujita, and Y. Sudo (2026) \n759\t DuplexCascade: Full-duplex speech-to-speech dialogue with vad-free cascaded asr-llm-tts pipeline and micro-turn optimization .\n760\t \n761\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.09180 .\n762\t \n763\t External Links: Link \n764\t \n765\t Cited by: §1 .\n766\t \n767\t \n768\t \n769\t C. Zhang, J. Chen, J. Li, Y. Peng, and Z. Mao (2023) \n770\t Large language models for human–robot interaction: a review .\n771\t \n772\t Biomimetic Intelligence and Robotics 3 ( 4 ), pp. 100131 .\n773\t \n774\t External Links: ISSN 2667-3797 ,\n775\t Document ,\n776\t Link \n777\t \n778\t Cited by: §1 .\n779\t \n780\t \n781\t \n782\t \n783\t \n784\t \n785\t \n786\t \n787\t \n788\t Experimental support, please\n789\t view the build logs \n790\t for errors. Generated by\n791\t \n792\t \n793\t L\n794\t A \n795\t T\n796\t E \n797\t \n798\t xml \n799\t \n800\t .\n801\t \n802\t \n803\t Instructions for reporting errors \n804\t We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile\n805\t support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the\n806\t methods listed below: \n807\t \n808\t Click the \"Report Issue\" ( \n809\t \n810\t ) button, located in the page header. \n811\t \n812\t Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. \n813\t Our team has already identified the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we\n814\t may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability\n815\t should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued